'' POKE'S DESTINATION IS AN INTEGER ADDRESS, AND THE STORE NEEDS A POINTER.
''
'' 'poke b, b' passes an INTEGER -- that is the whole point of POKE -- and it lowered
'' to an i64 temp used straight as the store's pointer operand:
''
''     %t950 = load i64, ptr %s45
''     store i8 %t952, ptr %t950
''
'' which clang refused with "'%t950' defined with type 'i64' but expected 'ptr'". No
'' object at all, for tests/vendor/fbc-tests/warnings/suffix-fb.bas among others.
''
'' PEEK ALREADY COERCED ITS ADDRESS the same way; the two halves of one intrinsic pair
'' gave different answers, and only the half nothing in the suite exercised was wrong.
''
'' The '@buf(2)' form is here beside the two integer ones because it takes the OTHER
'' branch -- a slot reference materialised through a zero-offset field address -- and
'' the fix must not disturb it.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/poke-integer-address.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' THE CONVERSION ITSELF. 'inttoptr' is what Coerce emits for the integer-to-pointer
'' direction, and asserting the store alone would pass again the moment the operand
'' went back to being an i64: the store TEXT is 'ptr %tN' either way.
'' EXPECT-OUT: = inttoptr i64
'' GOLDEN: expected/poke-integer-address.out
